---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";

// Stand-alone privacy policy for the mobile apps we publish on Google Play.
// This is the URL entered in Play Console's store listing, so it must be a
// complete policy on its own. The website has its own policy at /privacy
// (it runs analytics; this doesn't). Describes Delve, Die, Repeat 2.0
// (Leaderboards): Google Play Games leaderboards through the in-game Compete
// setting, on by default and switchable off at any time; and 2.1's in-app
// bug reports (☰ REPORT A BUG), sent only when the player taps Send, stored
// in the game's Firebase project and posted publicly on GitHub. The Play
// Console Data safety record lives in the game repo at
// store-listing/LISTING.md (mazeworld); the "Data safety summary" section
// below must match it.
const effective = "September 28, 2026";
// The address Google Play lists as the developer contact. Change here only.
const contact = "sheibeck@gmail.com";
---
<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy — Google Play apps"
  description="Privacy policy for Darktier Studios apps on Google Play, including Delve, Die, Repeat. Saves stay on your device; Google Play Games leaderboards run through the in-game Compete setting, which you can turn off at any time; a bug report is sent only when you tap Send."
>
  <div class="container">
    <section style="padding:84px 0 28px">
      <p class="kicker">Darktier Studios · legal · Google Play apps</p>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(40px,6vw,72px);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0 0 0 -0.06em">Privacy policy for our apps</h1>
      <p class="privacy-meta">Effective {effective} · {site.legalName}, {site.location}</p>
    </section>

    <article class="privacy">
      <p class="privacy-lede">
        The short version: <strong>we collect nothing automatically, and we run no servers of our
        own.</strong> Your runs, your graveyard, and your settings live on your device. There are two
        exceptions. <strong>Google Play Games leaderboards</strong> are controlled by the in-game
        <strong>Compete</strong> setting, which is on by default and which you can turn off at any time.
        With Compete off, the game sends no leaderboard data. And a <strong>bug report</strong> leaves your
        phone only if you write one and tap <strong>Send</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Who this covers</h2>
      <p>
        This policy applies to the mobile apps published by {site.legalName} on Google Play — currently
        <strong>Delve, Die, Repeat</strong> (package <code>com.darktierstudios.delvedierepeat</code>).
        Our website and browser-based tools are covered separately by the
        <a href="/privacy">website privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Data we collect</h2>
      <p>
        We — {site.legalName} — collect nothing automatically. We operate no servers of our own, no
        accounts, and no analytics. Our apps do not collect, transmit, sell, or share:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Personal information (name, email address, phone number, address)</li>
        <li>Location data, precise or approximate</li>
        <li>Financial or payment information</li>
        <li>Contacts, calendar, photos, videos, audio, or files</li>
        <li>Gameplay analytics, or automatic crash and diagnostic reports</li>
        <li>Device identifiers or the Android Advertising ID</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The game sends data in only two cases: the leaderboard data described in the next section, only
        while Compete is on; and a bug report you write yourself, only when you tap Send, described under
        <a href="#bug-reports">Bug reports</a>. Google's own Play Games software, which starts with the
        app, is covered by Google's privacy policy, as described below.
      </p>

      <h2>Leaderboards and Google Play Games (Compete)</h2>
      <p>
        Delve, Die, Repeat posts your finished runs to four <strong>leaderboards</strong> — deepest descent,
        longest-lived, most kills, and fattest purse — so you can see where you placed against
        other players and your friends. This is powered by <strong>Google Play Games Services</strong>, a
        feature of Google Play, and it runs only while the in-game <strong>Compete</strong> setting is on.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Compete is on by default.</strong> With Compete on, the game may sign you in to Google Play
          Games automatically when it starts, using the Play Games profile on your phone. Declining, or
          having no Play Games profile, leaves the game fully playable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>With Compete off,</strong> the game makes no sign-in, submission, or leaderboard request,
          and sends no leaderboard data. Google's own Play Games software, part of Google Play services,
          still starts with the app and may show its own "Welcome back" banner. That is governed by Google's
          privacy policy and your Play Games settings, not by the game.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What is sent while Compete is on:</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>your Google Play Games player ID;</li>
            <li>for each run that ends, the four leaderboard scores for that run;</li>
            <li>
              a short run summary: race, sub-class, level, cause of death, floor reached, days survived,
              steps walked, kills, wilmst carried, experience points, and your adventurer's name. The name
              is the one the game rolled for your adventurer, not yours, and it may be shortened.
            </li>
          </ul>
          Not sent: the epitaph, your saves, your settings, your personal bests, and your local graveyard
          stay on your phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Friends:</strong> only if you tap <strong>SHOW MY FRIENDS</strong> on the Leaderboards
          panel and allow it on Google's own screen does the game show your Play Games friends' scores for
          this game. Global and friends results are shown on your phone and not saved.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where it goes:</strong> to Google, not to us. Google stores it and displays it on the
          game's leaderboards under your Play Games profile name and avatar. It is visible to other players
          of the game, and to your Play Games friends if you use that feature. We have no server of our own
          and never receive a copy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google's rules apply.</strong> Play Games Services, your Play Games profile, and your
          friends list are governed by
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>
          and your <a href="https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/3129346" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Games profile settings</a>,
          where you control who can see your profile and activity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stopping and deleting:</strong> to stop, open the account menu (the small face at the top
          right of the title screen, or beside the ☰ menu in the dungeon) and choose
          <strong>STOP COMPETING</strong>, or set <strong>COMPETE</strong> to <strong>OFF</strong>. Turning
          Compete off also discards any runs still waiting to be sent. To disconnect the game from Play Games
          entirely, use the Play Games app. You can delete your Play Games data for this game at any time
          from your Play Games profile (in the Play Store's Play Games Profile settings, or at
          <a href="https://play.google.com/games/profile" target="_blank" rel="noopener">play.google.com/games/profile</a>).
          That erases your scores from the leaderboards. Step-by-step instructions:
          <a href="/privacy/delete-data">delete your data</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="bug-reports">Bug reports</h2>
      <p>
        Delve, Die, Repeat has a <strong>REPORT A BUG</strong> option in its ☰ menu. Nothing is sent unless
        you type a report and tap <strong>Send</strong>. A report contains what you typed, the game's in-run
        log (the Oracle, which may include your adventurer's randomly generated name), the app version, your
        device model and Android version, and a summary of the current run (floor, class, race, level and
        turn count). It contains no account, Google Play Games identity, email address or other contact
        detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reports are stored in the game's Google Firebase project and posted publicly as issues on the game's
        GitHub repository
        (<a href="https://github.com/sheibeck/ddr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/sheibeck/ddr</a>),
        where anyone can read them, so please leave out anything private. To have a report deleted, follow
        the steps on our <a href="/privacy/delete-data#bug-reports">delete your data</a> page or
        <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>contact us</a>, and we will delete it from Firebase and GitHub.
      </p>

      <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
      <p>
        The game keeps a small amount of data on your phone so it works: your current run, the graveyard of
        adventurers who came before it, your personal best records, your settings (including Compete), and,
        while you are offline, the short list of runs waiting to be sent to the leaderboards. None of it is
        sent to us, and it is removed when you uninstall the app.
      </p>

      <h2>No servers of our own, no ads, no analytics</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps require no account of our own and play fully offline. They contain no advertising,
        analytics, or crash-reporting SDKs. Google Play handles your purchase and installation, and Google
        Play Games Services handles the leaderboards, both under
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>.
        Google describes what its Play Games software collects on its own
        <a href="https://developer.android.com/games/pgs/data-collection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Games data disclosure page</a>.
        We receive no personal information from either. A bug report you choose to send goes to the game's
        Google Firebase project and is posted publicly on GitHub, as described under
        <a href="#bug-reports">Bug reports</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps do not request access to your location, contacts, camera, microphone, storage, or any other
        permission-gated data. The one permission is internet access, which is used only for Google Play
        Games while Compete is on, for sending a bug report when you tap Send, and for Google's own
        start-up described above.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps are suitable for general audiences. We collect no data automatically from anyone,
        including children under 13, and a bug report carries no account or contact detail. Leaderboards need a Google Play Games profile, and Google's own age rules and parental
        controls govern whether a child can create one or appear on leaderboards. The game needs no sign-in
        to be played, and Compete can be turned off at any time.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        We hold no account or profile about you. Everything the app stores on its own lives on your device
        under your control; uninstalling the app deletes it. Leaderboard data sent while Compete was on is
        held by Google and can be reviewed or deleted through your Play Games profile as described above.
        The only thing we hold is a bug report you chose to send; ask, and we will delete it from Firebase
        and GitHub. If a future app ever changes any of
        this, this policy will change first.
      </p>

      <h2>Data safety summary</h2>
      <p>
        For the Google Play Data safety section, Delve, Die, Repeat declares: <strong>User IDs</strong> (the
        Play Games player ID) and <strong>App activity</strong> (the run scores and the short run summary)
        are collected. They are optional (turn Compete off), used for <strong>App functionality</strong>,
        encrypted in transit, and deletable by you. They are not shared with third parties and not sold.
        <strong>Other user-generated content</strong> (the bug
        report you type) and <strong>Diagnostics</strong> (the Oracle log, app version, device model and
        Android version, and run summary attached to it) are collected only when you tap Send, used for
        <strong>App functionality</strong>, and deleted on request; a report is posted publicly on GitHub as
        described above, and not sold. Nothing else is collected.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If we update this policy, the new version will be posted at this address with a new effective date.
        Material changes to what an app collects will also be reflected in that app's Google Play listing.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a><br />
        {site.legalName} · {site.location}
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
